-- title:
--   The Drinfeld function field is a curve over k
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a natural number and $k$ a field, and suppose that the coordinate ring `CoordRing q k`, namely the quotient of the polynomial ring $k[X_0,X_1]$ in two variables (indexed by `Fin 2`) by the principal ideal generated by `drinfeldPoly q k` $- 1$, is an integral domain; the latter hypothesis in particular makes its fraction field `drinfeldFunctionField q k` a field. The assertion is that this fraction field $F$ is a curve over $k$ in the sense of the class [`AlgebraicCurve.IsCurveOver`](def/AlgebraicCurve_IsCurveOver.html#L15), i.e. three things hold. First, principal divisors have degree zero: for every nonzero $f \in F$ there is a divisor $D$ of $F/k$ whose value at each place equals the order $\operatorname{ord}_v f$ and whose degree is $0$, where a place is a valuation subring of $F$ that contains the image of $k$, is not all of $F$, and is a principal ideal ring. Secondly, for every such place $v$ the residue field of $v$ is a finite $k$-module. Thirdly, the module $\Omega[F\,/\,k]$ of Kähler differentials is free over $F$ of rank $1$.
--
--   This identifies the function field of the affine Drinfeld curve as a curve over its base field, so that the general divisor-theoretic machinery (places, divisors, degrees, differentials) applies to it. It is used in the study of the divisor class group of this function field, for instance in the computation recorded in [`DrinfeldCurve.abelJacobiCard_drinfeldFunctionField`](thm.html#DrinfeldCurve.abelJacobiCard_drinfeldFunctionField), and in further results depending on it.
